      <h1>🏇 Horseback Riding</h1>
    <p>Riding a horse is one of the most magical experiences a person can have. It's not just sport — it's a partnership between human and animal built on trust, balance, and communication.</p>

    <h2>🐾 Why Ride Horses?</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>It builds a deep bond between human and animal</li>
        <li>It improves balance, posture, and coordination</li>
        <li>It boosts confidence and emotional well-being</li>
        <li>It connects you with nature in a unique way</li>
        <li>It's a full-body workout — even if it doesn't feel like it!</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>🧭 Types of Horseback Riding</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>English Riding:</strong> Used in competitions and shows. Riders sit upright with a flat saddle. Includes dressage, show jumping, and cross-country.
            <br />
            <img width="20%" src="Pics/EnglishRidingPic.png" alt="English Riding" style="margin-top:8px; border-radius:8px;" />
        </li>
        <li style="margin-top:12px;">
            <strong>Western Riding:</strong> Common in the USA, with a large comfortable saddle. Often seen in rodeos and cowboy culture.
            <br />
            <img width="20%" src="Pics/WesternRidingPic.png" alt="Western Riding" style="margin-top:8px; border-radius:8px;" />
        </li>
        <li style="margin-top:12px;">
            <strong>Trail Riding:</strong> Riding through forests and fields. Perfect for beginners and outdoor adventures.
            <br />
            <img width="20%" src="Pics/TrailRidingPic.png" alt="Trail Riding" style="margin-top:8px; border-radius:8px;" />
        </li>
        <li style="margin-top:12px;">
            <strong>Dressage:</strong> Often called "horse ballet" — focuses on precision and elegant movement between horse and rider.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-top:12px;">
            <strong>Show Jumping:</strong> Riders guide their horse over obstacles in a specific order. Requires speed and accuracy.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>🔰 Beginner Tips</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Always wear a certified riding helmet and proper boots</li>
        <li>Take lessons with a qualified instructor before riding alone</li>
        <li>Approach the horse calmly from the left side</li>
        <li>Learn walk, halt, turn, and trot before cantering</li>
        <li>Always warm up the horse before intense riding</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>🧠 The Mental Side of Riding</h2>
    <p>Horses feel your emotions through your body. If you're tense, your horse will feel it too. The best riders are calm, focused, and confident. Riding teaches patience, empathy, and self-control.</p>

    <h2>📸 My Dream</h2>
    <p>One day, I hope to ride regularly — maybe even compete or own a horse. Until then, every ride is a step closer to that dream.</p>

</asp:Content>
